1050 420.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176122 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "5b617917-9881-594e-8972-61a82f2871a8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176508 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186175186 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176508 #edb/ref 1069 "q-50897" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176508 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176508 #edb/ref 1071 "What is the sum of the series $-2+\\frac{8}{3!}-\\frac{32}{5!}+⋯+\\frac{(-1)^{n}(-2)^{2n+1}}{(2n+1)!}+⋯\\,?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176508 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "b610a1ae-13a4-52d6-8097-cd81c48ac52e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176508 #edb/ref 1074 "We recognize that the series looks similar to the Maclaurin expansion for $f(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & =x-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3!}+\\frac{{x}^{5}}{5!}-⋯+\\frac{(-1)^{n}{x}^{2n+1}}{(2n+1)!}+⋯\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=-2$ into our Maclaurin expansion gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-2) & =(-2)-\\frac{(-2)^{3}}{3!}+\\frac{(-2)^{5}}{5!}-⋯+\\frac{(-1)^{n}(-2)^{2n+1}}{(2n+1)!}+⋯ \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡2 & =-2+\\frac{8}{3!}-\\frac{32}{5!}+⋯+\\frac{(-1)^{n}(-2)^{2n+1}}{(2n+1)!}+⋯\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the sum of the series is $-\\operatorname{sin}⁡2.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176510 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186175198 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176510 #edb/ref 1069 "q-50935" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176510 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176510 #edb/ref 1071 "What is the sum of the series $\\frac{\\pi }{4}-\\frac{(\\pi /4)^{3}}{3!}+\\frac{(\\pi /4)^{5}}{5!}-⋯+\\frac{(-1)^{n}(\\pi /4)^{2n+1}}{(2n+1)!}+⋯\\,?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176510 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "ad9b9f3f-7dff-5b1d-aff9-251f7b31c672" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176510 #edb/ref 1074 "We recognize that the series looks similar to the Maclaurin expansion for $f(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & =x-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3!}+\\frac{{x}^{5}}{5!}-⋯+\\frac{(-1)^{n}{x}^{2n+1}}{(2n+1)!}+⋯\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ into our Maclaurin expansion gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}-\\frac{(\\pi /4)^{3}}{3!}+\\frac{(\\pi /4)^{5}}{5!}-\\frac{(\\pi /4)^{7}}{7!}+⋯+\\frac{(-1)^{n}(\\pi /4)^{2n+1}}{(2n+1)!}+⋯ \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}-\\frac{(\\pi /4)^{3}}{3!}+\\frac{(\\pi /4)^{5}}{5!}-\\frac{(\\pi /4)^{7}}{7!}+⋯+\\frac{(-1)^{n}(\\pi /4)^{2n+1}}{(2n+1)!}+⋯\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the sum of the series is $\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176676 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186176677 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176676 #edb/ref 1061 649412 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176676 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "02032486-ef93-5b31-a042-ff9aedc94caf" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176676 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186175950 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176677 #edb/ref 1061 649415 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176677 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "ded33c8c-6f4b-5349-ade4-f49fb9e69602" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176677 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186175951 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176693 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186176676 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176693 #edb/ref 1061 649411 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176693 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "ed13fe45-d03c-55c5-912d-7530fab1d9cd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176693 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186176979 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176979 #edb/ref 1064 "We need to recognize when a particular series looks like a standard Maclaurin series. A list of some common Maclaurin series is given below.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\frac{1}{1-x}=1+x+{x}^{2}+⋯+{x}^{n}+⋯={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{x}^{n}, &  & x\\in (-1,1) \\\\  & \\frac{1}{1+x}=1-x+{x}^{2}-⋯+(-1)^{n}{x}^{n}+⋯={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }(-1)^{n}{x}^{n}, &  & x\\in (-1,1) \\\\  & {e}^{x}=1+x+\\frac{1}{2!}{x}^{2}+⋯+\\frac{{x}^{n}}{n!}+⋯={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }\\frac{{x}^{n}}{n!}, &  & x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ) \\\\  & \\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+x)=x-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}-⋯+\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}{x}^{n}}{n}+⋯={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}{x}^{n}}{n}, &  & x\\in (-1,1] \\\\  & \\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)=x-\\frac{1}{3!}{x}^{3}+\\frac{1}{5!}{x}^{5}+⋯+\\frac{(-1)^{n}{x}^{2n+1}}{(2n+1)!}+⋯={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n}{x}^{2n+1}}{(2n+1)!}, &  & x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ) \\\\  & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)=1-\\frac{1}{2!}{x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{4!}{x}^{4}-⋯+\\frac{(-1)^{n}{x}^{2n}}{(2n)!}+⋯={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n}{x}^{2n}}{(2n)!}, &  & x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this lesson, we will focus on recognizing the standard Maclaurin series for the sine and cosine functions.\n\n**Note:** To remember the series for $\\sin x$ and $\\cos x$ easily, notice that they are both alternating series that contain every other term of the expansion for $e^x.$ Sine has the odd-degree terms (since it is an odd function), and cosine has the even-degree terms (since it is an even function)." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176979 #edb/ref 1065 5193 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176979 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "d665f747-9ba7-5bcf-bb89-b9f7cf00815f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176979 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186538246 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{1+\\sqrt{3}}{2}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186538246 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186538246 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "058b950b-7211-5b63-a8ef-ca5da43d6e22" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186538295 #edb/ref 1080 "\\operatorname{ln}⁡2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186538295 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186538295 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "1c0c8ec8-308a-5fdc-8743-e14424b39b08" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186538297 #edb/ref 1080 "-\\frac{1}{2}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [1759